---
title: 'iPhone Duo: the foldable test is software'
description: 'Apple’s first foldable has striking hardware. Its lasting value will depend on continuity, multitasking, app design, and what happens after the novelty fades.'
category: technology
status: published
publishedAt: '2026-10-11'
sources:
  - title: 'Apple: Introducing iPhone Duo'
    url: https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/09/apple-unveils-iphone-duo/
---
Apple's first foldable iPhone arrives on 23 October, so this is an examination of the announcement rather than a review. The hardware is easy to picture: a compact outer screen opens into a 7.6-inch inner display. The harder question is whether the software makes that transformation useful after the first week.

[Apple describes](https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/09/apple-unveils-iphone-duo/) a 5.4-inch outer display, a 7.6-inch inner display, A20 Pro, two batteries, and a titanium frame and hinge cover. Pre-orders begin on 16 October. Durability, repairability, the visible crease, and real battery behaviour need independent testing once shipping devices are available.

## A larger screen needs a reason to exist

A foldable can fail in two opposite ways. It can stretch a phone app across more pixels without adding value, or it can become so desktop-like that it loses the directness of a phone.

iOS 27 introduces side-by-side Split View, saved app pairs, two windows from the same app, and layouts that respond as the device folds or rotates. Apple also says its own apps are adapted and that developers receive updated APIs and tools.

The best experiences will use the inner display for relationships that are awkward on a normal phone: a document beside its references, a call beside shared material, a map beside the details of a journey. More content is only useful when the hierarchy remains clear.

## Continuity is the real interaction

Opening the device should preserve context. The same task needs to move between screens without losing scroll position, selection, playback, or an unfinished input. Controls must remain reachable, and an app needs a sensible state when the hinge is partly open.

These transitions sound small, but they determine whether the fold feels like one adaptable product or two displays connected by a hinge. Developers will need to test more than responsive width. Posture, orientation, simultaneous windows, keyboards, and interrupted transitions all become part of the interface.

## AI will make the permissions question sharper

Apple presents the larger screen as a place to keep Siri AI visible beside an app. It says Siri can use personal context, understand onscreen content, and take action across apps, with a mix of on-device processing and Private Cloud Compute.

That can reduce friction, but it also puts permission design in the foreground. People should be able to see which app and information the assistant is using, preview consequential actions, and revoke access without searching through layers of settings.

Foldables do not need to replace ordinary phones to matter. They can establish a new interaction space between phone and tablet. The iPhone Duo will be convincing when opening it changes what a person can comfortably do, not merely how large the same interface appears.

